  "live_records": {
    "q-143052": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143052",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=(x-1)^{2}-1$ for $x\\le 1.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=(x-1)^{2}-1\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx=(y-1)^{2}-1\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(y-1)^{2}-1 \\\\ x+1 & =(y-1)^{2} \\\\ -\\sqrt{x+1} & =y-1 \\\\ 1-\\sqrt{x+1} & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we take the negative square root because if $x\\le 1$ is the domain of $f,$ then the range of the inverse is ${f}^{-1}\\le 1.$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with ${f}^{-1}(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=1-\\sqrt{x+1}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
